{
  "meta": {
    "title": "Low Tide Window: Alginate Lysis in a Tide-Pool Biofilm",
    "date": "2026-10-07",
    "lesson": "At low tide on a rocky shore, Pseudoalteromonas and Zobellia biofilms on brown-algal fronds (rockweed, kelp holdfasts) are exposed to air, desiccation, UV and temperature swings for hours at a time. They survive by hunkering down in EPS glue and switching to dormancy, then snap back to active alginate-degrading life the moment the tide floods the pool again. This level makes the tidal clock itself the main hazard: food (mostly algal cell-wall carbohydrate, with some lipid and protein from diatom epiphytes) blooms when the pool is flooded and nearly vanishes when it drains, grazers (harpacticoid-copepod-like protists) crowd the shrinking pool water, and a founder that cannot encyst fast enough simply cooks. The teaching point is EPS and dormancy as survival strategies against a predictable but brutal physical cycle, not against another organism.",
    "citation": "Intertidal biofilm ecology; Pseudoalteromonas/Zobellia alginate lyase systems on Fucus/Laminaria surfaces (inspired by studies of intertidal rock-pool microbiomes)",
    "difficulty": "hard",
    "realWorldBasis": "Intertidal rock-pool biofilms on brown algae, Pacific Northwest / Brittany coast tide pools"
  },
